# Substrate install inputs — copy to .env beside the docker-compose.yml you
# launch, and set what your node needs. In a checkout that is the REPO ROOT (the
# real docker-compose.yml; this directory's copy is a symlink to it), which is
# the only .env `make up` reads. Values exported in the shell outrank the .env.
# The .env file is gitignored; no launcher consults client config files or CLI
# credential caches.
#
# Setup commands: README § Installation. Every other variable the manifest
# forwards is advanced configuration, documented once in
# docs/operations/CONFIGURATION_SURFACE.md.
#
# Required inputs per profile:
#   standalone  1  a provider key
#   spoke, surface, compute  2  DISCOVERY_ENDPOINT + METABOB_API_KEY
#   hub  3  PROFILE=hub, a provider key, PUBLIC_IP
#
# What the running container actually resolved, and where each value came from:
#   docker exec <container> substrate-config

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Instance name and ports
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SUBSTRATE_NAME names the container <name>-live and the volumes <name>-workspace
# and <name>-surreal. The default reproduces substrate-live / substrate-workspace
# / substrate-surreal, so an existing default install is adopted as-is.
# SUBSTRATE_NAME=substrate
#
# Host port = prefix followed by the last three digits of the container port:
# 18 -> 18080, 18100, 18310, 18333 … A second fleet on one host takes a different
# name AND prefix; prefixes 19-32 stay out of the ephemeral port range.
# SUBSTRATE_PORT_PREFIX=18

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LLM provider — a standalone or hub needs one; a spoke needs none
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Left commented deliberately: a placeholder that is merely non-empty boots a
# fleet that cannot draft a single token. Set a REAL key or leave it unset.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#
# OpenAI-compatible alternative (Ollama, vLLM, LM Studio, …):
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
# LLM_DEFAULT_MODEL=llama3.2

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Join a hub (spoke, surface, compute)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These two are the whole join: the role, the hub's identity and trace-store
# endpoints and the relay anchor are derived from them in the image. The key is
# issued BY THE HUB:  docker exec <hub-container> substrate-key issue <this-node>
# DISCOVERY_ENDPOINT=http://<hub-host>:18100
# METABOB_API_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Profile — which vessels run
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# standalone (default with no anchor) | spoke (default with a remote anchor) |
# hub | hub-minimal | surface | compute
# PROFILE=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Hub
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The address spokes reach; discovery's /bootstrap advertises it.
# PUBLIC_IP=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Self-development (push capability and its scope)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A token with write access grants the substrate the capability to land its own
# commits; SUBSTRATE_REPO_OWNER scopes those landings to that owner's
# repositories (normally your own fork). A new install with a token and no owner
# is refused.
# SUBSTRATE_GIT_PAT=
# SUBSTRATE_REPO_OWNER=
#
# Emergency stop for autonomous landing (a kill switch, not the autonomy
# setting): 0 = no commit, no push, no host-sync intent (refusal kind
# push_kill_switch); unset or 1 = landings allowed, scoped by SUBSTRATE_REPO_OWNER
# and the pushPolicy impulse. It takes effect on the next recreate (README
# § Installation, Usage patterns: "Change an install input").
# MITOSIS_DIRECT_PUSH=0

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Exposure (advanced) — which host address each port is published on
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unset publishes on every host interface. SUBSTRATE_PUBLISH_IP applies to every
# port; <VESSEL>_PUBLISH_IP (ACTIVITY_API, DEV_VESSEL, DISCOVERY, IDENTITY,
# GOAL_HOST, ANALYSIS, CONCEPT_DB, STATEFUL_UI, HUMAN_SURFACE, RELAY) outranks it
# for one port. A human surface kept on the local host only:
# SUBSTRATE_PUBLISH_IP=127.0.0.1
#
# The relay announces the port it is published on. RELAY_ANNOUNCE_PORT is only
# for peers that dial a different port (e.g. the container port 30333 across a
# shared container network).
# RELAY_ANNOUNCE_PORT=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Deprecated names — still honoured, each warned at boot with its replacement
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Kept only so an existing fleet recreates onto the same container, volumes and
# ports. A deprecated name that conflicts with its replacement is refused rather
# than guessed; so is a partial set of the three name aliases on a new install
# (an install already running on its volumes gets a warning instead).
#   SUBSTRATE_CONTAINER, WORKSPACE_VOLUME, SURREAL_VOLUME   -> SUBSTRATE_NAME
#     (to adopt a fleet by exact names, set all three)
#   ACTIVITY_API_PORT, DEV_VESSEL_PORT, DISCOVERY_PORT, IDENTITY_PORT,
#   GOAL_HOST_PORT, ANALYSIS_PORT, CONCEPT_DB_PORT, STATEFUL_UI_PORT,
#   HUMAN_SURFACE_PORT                                      -> SUBSTRATE_PORT_PREFIX
#   (make) LIVE_NAME -> SUBSTRATE_NAME,  PORT_OFFSET -> SUBSTRATE_PORT_PREFIX
#   RELAY_PORT (advanced) keeps an existing hub's relay on the host port its
#     spokes already dial, instead of <prefix>333.
